\par\par \b \uc2 *\uc2 Google\uc2  Investing About $75 Million in Film Studio A24, Sources Say -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 754 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 June 2026\par 16:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2026, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 22 Jun 2026 10:00 ET *A24 and \uc2 Google DeepMind\uc2  Establish AI Research Partnership, Companies Say -- WSJ\par \par \uc2 22 Jun 2026 10:00 ET *Nonexclusive, Multiyear Deal to Include Development of AI Filmmaking Tools, Companies Say -- WSJ\par \par \uc2 22 Jun 2026 10:00 ET  \uc2 Google\uc2  Investing in 'Backrooms' Studio A24 -- WSJ\par \par \uc2 By Ben Fritz\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  is investing in independent movie studio A24 as part of a new \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial-intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2  research partnership between the two companies.\par \par \uc2 The search giant is putting around $75 million into A24, which released the recent hits "Backrooms" and "Marty Supreme," according to people familiar with the matter. Though \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  unit \uc2 Google\uc2  is a major player in online entertainment through \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , the deal marks the first time it has taken a stake in a studio.\par \par \uc2 A24 last raised funds in 2024, when \uc2 Thrive Capital\uc2  led a round valuing it at $3.5 billion. \uc2 Google\uc2 's investment is roughly equal to what Thrive put in at the time, the people familiar with the matter said. A24's revenue has more than doubled in the past two years as it has increased the budgets of films it makes and expanded its businesses like unscripted television, music and theater.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2 's DeepMind AI unit and A24 are aiming to create new tools for movie production and distribution. Their alliance is an unusual one between well-known names in industries that have eyed each other warily since the advent of models that can generate audio, images and video. Studios have sued and sent threatening letters to AI companies for alleged copyright violations and filmmakers have decried AI as an enemy of creativity.\par \par \uc2 Disney\uc2  signed a partnership with \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  last year that abruptly ended when the tech company shut down its Sora video tool in March. \uc2 Netflix\uc2  recently acquired an AI startup founded by Ben Affleck that can tweak scenes without reshoots. But by and large, mainstream Hollywood has done little with AI thus far.\par \par \uc2 Scott Belsky\uc2 , an A24 partner who oversees its technology and innovation work, said that is because developers have pushed AI as a way to make movies faster and cheaper, which doesn't appeal to filmmakers.\par \par \uc2 "We think there are better uses that preserve creative control and support risk-taking," he said. The new tools "won't look anything like the prompted generation type of AI that people feel uncomfortable with."\par \par \uc2 Their multiyear, nonexclusive deal doesn't give \uc2 Google\uc2  access to A24's data, including its film and television library.\par \par \uc2 The new collaborators hope their partnership will include the studio's roster of artists. A24 works with some of the biggest names in Hollywood including Timoth\u233\'00\'E9e Chalamet and "Backrooms" director Kane Parsons.\par \par \uc2 "We believe breakthroughs happen when you get technology into the hands of the best minds in the field," said Eli Collins, a vice president of product for DeepMind.\par \par \uc2 A24 and DeepMind have been in talks since before Belsky joined the studio from \uc2 Adobe\uc2  last year.\par \par \uc2 His 20-person team, called A24 Labs, is already developing an application for AI-generated storyboards -- roughly drawn drafts of a movie that can help identify problems before production begins.\par \par \uc2 A24 has grown rapidly since releasing its first movie in 2013. It now stands alongside \uc2 Disney\uc2  as one of the few studios that draws people to theaters based on its name alone. A little more than half of moviegoers consider themselves fans of A24, according to data from research firm NRG shared by the studio.\par \par \uc2 Partnering with \uc2 Google\uc2  is a significant and potentially risky move for A24. Its reputation is derived in large part from its alignment with emerging filmmakers and young audiences -- exactly the people often skeptical of tech conglomerates and AI's impact on society.\par \par \uc2 The deal comes as the New York-based company has been taking a number of steps to try to grow into a global media company without losing its edge.\par \par \uc2 A24's leaders have been meeting potential strategic investors overseas that could help it to produce international movies and series, according to people familiar with the matter. It has already established an office in the U.K. to produce content there.\par \par \uc2 The studio is currently making its most expensive movie ever, a roughly $175 million adaptation of the videogame "Elden Ring." It is directed by Alex Garland, who previously made "Civil War" and "Ex Machina" for A24.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ben Fritz at ben.fritz@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 22, 2026 10:00 ET (14:00 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Document DJDN000020260622em6m001wt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
