rmation in relation to the \par Announcement. \par  \par     Certain factors in relation to the Consideration for the Acquisition \par In relation to certain factors (the "Strategic Factors") referred to in the Announcement as \par having been taken into account in relation to the Consideration for the Acquisition, the \par Company wishes to supplement as follows: \par  \par (i)     Growing demand for high-quality AI data training and annotation services required for \par the development, fine-tuning and continuous improvement of AI models \par The Company noted that market demand was continuing to show increase in relation to \par high-quality data training and annotation services regarding developing, fine-tuning \par and continuously improving AI models, particularly as AI adoption was seen to be \par accelerating across many industries. \par  \par     In addition to Slencor AI's unaudited accounts for the full-year ended 31 December \par 2025 ("FY2025"), the Company noted that Slencor AI's management accounts for the \par three months ended 31 March 2026 ("1Q2026") recorded unaudited net profit of \par approximately HK$2.7 million ("1Q2026 Net Profit"); however, since quarterly \par results would not necessarily be indicative of full-year performance, the Company \par noted them as an additional data point, and did not rely upon them on a standalone basis \par as a factor in arriving at the Consideration. \par  \par (ii)    Positioning of Slencor AI in the emerging field of AI agentic data training and RLHF \par business \par The Company noted that AI agentic data training and RLHF were increasingly \par important components in developing, optimising and deploying advanced AI models, \par that the increasing complexity and sophistication of AI applications were driving \par growing demand for such high-quality, human-guided data training and model \par refinement services, and that, relative to many early-stage technology companies, \par Slencor AI had demonstrated profitability, which the Company considered as \par demonstrating a degree of commercial traction and business model scalability. \par  \par (iii)   Experience and capabilities of management and operating team \par The Company noted the aggregate of approximately 4 years of research and \par development and commercialised operations of Slencor AI prior to the Acquisition, as \par well as the management and operating team's delivery of profitability across relevant \par periods and expertise in AI-related technology, operational delivery, and business \par development, and that it was expected that such industry experience and execution \par capability would be support the future development potential and scalability of the \par business. \par  \par (iv)    Alignment of Slencor AI's capabilities with the Group's broader technology strategy to \par enhance its technology capabilities and support its digital transformation initiatives \par The Company noted that Slencor AI's capabilities aligned closely with the Group's \par broader strategy to enhance its AI, data intelligence and technology offerings and \par accelerate digital transformation initiatives, thereby strengthening the Company's \par technology-for-business solutions ecosystem. \par  \par (v)     Potential to enhance the Group's competitive position in the AI agentic data training \par sector \par The Company noted that the Acquisition would be expected to enhance the Group's \par competitive position in the AI agentic data training sector and strengthen its overall \par exposure to AI-related market opportunities, and that participation in the AI data \par training and RLHF sector would enable the Group to strengthen its market positioning \par within the broader AI value chain, improving its ability to capture high-growth \par commercial opportunities as enterprise adoption of AI continues to increase. \par  \par (vi)      Potential for cross-selling AI-related solutions to the Group's existing customer base \par Given the increasing demand for AI data training and RLHF-related services across \par multiple sectors, the Company noted that the Group's existing business networks and \par commercial relationships would provide a foundation for collaboration and business \par development, allowing for relatively seamless integration of AI-related capabilities \par across the Group's technology platforms and solutions offerings, leveraging the \par Group's ecosystem to support future expansion. \par  \par     Price/Earnings ("P/E") multiple comparables in relation to the Acquisition \par The Consideration for the Acquisition translated into a P/E multiple of approximately 16.2x \par based on Slencor AI's net profit for FY2025. For comparison purposes, the Company reviewed \par the P/E multiples of comparable listed companies. \par  \par     Slencor AI's business encompasses AI and related technology investments and operations, \par including: (i) AI agentic training business, with Reinforcement Learning from Human \par Feedback (RLHF); (ii) AI-related expert talent recruitment; and (iii) AI-related marketplace. \par  \par     Given the highly specialised nature of Slencor AI's business, the Company had not been able \par to identify identical pure-play comparable companies listed on the Stock Exchange. \par  \par     Accordingly, to conduct a comparison of P/E multiples, the Company performed a database \par screening on the Stock Exchange website to identify a fair and representative benchmark group \par as of the Comparison Date for this purpose, pursuant to which 11 companies were identified \par based on the following criteria: \par Sector & Core Business: The comparable companies were selected from the Software & \par Services sub-sector within the Information Technology sector. The selection process \par focused on peer companies providing application software and digital solution services, \par rather than less comparable sub-sectors such as gaming software, internet services, and \par infrastructure. Furthermore, the core operations of the selected comparable companies \par mainly or substantially comprised the provision of AI-related services, spanning. \par  \par     enterprise-level AI agentic software, 3D spatial AI, artificial IoT, AI-driven enterprise \par solutions, AI business outsourcing, AI staffing and recruitment, training empowerment, \par enterprise SaaS, enterprise RaaS, AI agent operating systems, or AI-related consulting and \par design; \par Trading Status: Its shares were traded on the Stock Exchange and had not been suspended \par from trading for more than 30 consecutive trading days as at the Comparison Date; and \par Profitability: Its latest published full-year accounts as at the Comparison Date reported a \par net profit, facilitating a valid P/E multiple calculation. \par  \par     The Comparison Date was 28 April 2026, being the trading day immediately preceding the \par Agreement Date. All P/E multiple calculations were derived strictly from closing share prices \par on the Comparison Date and the latest available published full-year net profit as of that date. \par  \par     These P/E multiples are set out below: \par Market                         P/E \par Company Name                                             Capitalisation      Net Profit   multiple \par (Stock Code)      Includes Relevant Business             (HK$ million)    (HK$ million)        (x) \par Kingdee           Engaged in enterprise cloud services        29,212.1             84.6     345.5 \par International     and enterprise resource planning \par Software Group    (ERP) business, and software \par Company Limited   solution consulting, including its \par  \par (268)             Kingdee Cosmic AI, an agentic \par enterprise AI platform engaged in \par finance, HR & operations \par Marketingforce    Engaged in AI application business,           9,415.4            84.0     112.1 \par Management        providing cloud-based marketing \par Limited           and sales services and intelligent \par  \par (2556)            talent management services, \par generative AI operating system \par business, and precision marketing \par services \par Ming Yuan Cloud   Engaged in AI-integrated enterprise           3,782.0            34.8     108.8 \par Group Holdings    cloud services and Software as a \par Limited           Service and Platform as a Service, \par  \par (909)             including CRM Cloud AI products \par and AI modules in investment \par management, project development \par and asset management sectors \par BoardWare         Engaged in providing enterprise IT             825.0              8.1     101.3 \par Intelligence      solutions and consultancy, \par Technology        including core AI platforms - \par Limited           BoardWare AI Unify Framework \par  \par (1204)            and TerraMind AI Agent Platform \par Maiyue            Engaged in AI-integrated IT                    575.0              6.2       92.2 \par Technology        solution services, including cloud- \par Limited           based computing resources for \par  \par (2501)            large-scale AI model training, and a \par vertically integrated hardware - \par software - algorithm solution \par engineered for rapid AI deployment \par and inference \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 22 Jun 2026 10:51 ET \par HK Bourse: Announcement From Vongroup Ltd -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par GL-Carlink        Engaged in Software as a Service              1,965.0            25.0       78.5 \par Technology        (Saas) in the automotive industry, \par Holding Limited   including an intelligent connected \par  \par (2531)            value-added service cloud and an \par in-vehicle system utilising neural \par networks and AI to offer intelligent \par driver-assistance and connected \par services \par Bairong Inc.          Engaged in providing AI                        3,365.0              84.7        39.8 \par  \par (6608)                employees to institutional clients \par through Results-as-a-Service \par  \par (RaaS), enterprise-level intelligent \par agent operating system, intelligent \par agent powered AI employees, AI \par business outsourcing (AI BPO), AI \par silicon-based staffing, recruitment \par operations and training \par empowerment \par Chinasoft             Engaged in IT solutions and IT                 9,371.0             370.7        25.3 \par International         outsourcing, providing customers \par Limited               with one-stop AI service operations \par  \par (354)                 including computing power \par resource leasing, model training, \par data engineering, and scenario \par application development \par Tuya Inc              Engaged in AI agent engines, AI               10,916.4             451.5        24.2 \par  \par (2391)                agent development platform, \par integration of AI with the physical \par world, cloud computing, 3D spatial \par intelligence, and artificial Internet \par of Things developer platform \par Sinohealth            Engaged in AI-driven digital and               2,267.9             108.3        20.9 \par Technology            intelligent solutions for the \par Limited               healthcare industry and Software as \par  \par (2361)                a Service (SaaS) \par JF SmartInvest        Engaged in AI and big data                    15,062.4           1,056.4        14.3 \par Holdings Limited      technology, including an integrated \par  \par (9636)                AI + content + service + tools" \par solution \par Median                                                                                                78.5 \par Mean                                                                                                  87.5 \par Slencor AI                                                                                            16.2 \par Source: based primarily on publicly-available information on the websites of the Stock Exchange. Business \par relevance descriptions are summarised to highlight some of the main or substantial businesses that relate closely \par to the business characteristics of Slencor AI. \par  \par     The Company noted that, given that it is generally acknowledged that public market P/E \par multiples tend to inherently reflect future growth velocity and monetisation potential derived \par from core capabilities rather than corporate age alone, the Company considered that the median \par and mean P/E multiples of these comparable companies would be generally accepted to be \par objective, data-driven benchmarks for assessing the P/E multiple of the Consideration for the \par Acquisition. \par  \par     The Company also noted Slencor AI's unlisted status and its specific operational history, in \par comparison with the comparable companies. Taking this into account, the Company noted that \par the P/E multiple for the Acquisition (approximately 16.2x) represented a significant discount \par to the P/E multiples of the comparable companies, that is, approximately 79.4% discount to the \par median of the comparable companies' P/E multiples (which was approximately 78.5x) and \par approximately 80.1% discount to the mean of the comparable companies' P/E multiples (which \par was approximately 87.5x). \par  \par     Certain information in relation to the Disposal \par VCFC was primarily engaged in the investment and management of consumer finance assets, \par including accounts receivable and other financial assets, as well as the provision of consultancy \par services in relation to consumer finance-related matters. In light of current global economic \par turbulence, regional macroeconomic challenges, and dramatic structural shifts in consumer \par spending patterns, the Company believed that the Disposal was an appropriate decision that \par aligns with the best interests of the Company and its shareholders as a whole. \par  \par     In assessing the basis for the consideration of the Disposal, the Company had considered the \par nature of VCFC's business, its financial performance, and its future prospects. \par  \par     Unlike AI and technology businesses where value may generally be primarily driven by future \par growth potential, scalability, and intangible assets, the Company took that view that, in view \par of VCFC's continual subdued financial performance, its value was more closely tied to existing \par assets rather than future business. Accordingly, the Company took the view that the net asset \par value functioned as a unified balance sheet point of reference that maximised value, providing \par an appropriate and objective reference point having regard to the nature of its business. \par  \par     The Disposal consideration represented an implied Price-to-Book multiple of approximately \par  \par 1.04x, representing a premium of approximately 4.3% above VCFC's most recent unaudited \par net asset value ("NAV"), and secured a gain of approximately HK$1 million for the Group. \par  \par     The following illustrates the Company's assessment of certain strategic factors, amongst others, \par in relation to the Disposal: \par  \par (i)    Net asset value of VCFC and realisation at a premium \par According to the accounts of VCFC for its most recent financial year, VCFC recorded \par no net profit, therefore a P/E multiple was not applicable for the assessment of the \par Disposal consideration. \par  \par     However, by securing consideration that exceeds the NAV in relation to a loss-making \par non-core asset, the Company noted that it was able to successfully realise liquidity at a \par premium. Securing such premium provided certainty of the gain, maximised the net \par asset value of VCFC, and reflected a commercially reasonable basis for consideration \par for the Disposal. Consequently, the Disposal allows the Group to re-allocate its capital \par and strategic focus toward higher-yielding, core growth areas, specifically the AI \par business footprint that the Group expected to be expanded. \par  \par (ii)   Historical financial performance and future prospects of VCFC \par The Company noted the historical financial performance of VCFC, with relatively \par subdued financial performance in recent years and an absence of net profit. VCFC \par belongs to a relatively mature division with fairly low capital efficiency and low \par expansion potential, is a non-core asset, and does not form a part of the core operations \par of the Company. \par  \par     The Company took the view that the future growth potential and strategic fit of VCFC \par was relatively limited when compared with the Group's increasing focus on potentially \par higher-growth sectors, that is, AI and technology businesses. In this regard, the \par Disposal represented an opportunity for the Group to realise value from a non-core \par business, achieve a clean exit, and re-deploy resources towards toward potentially \par higher-yielding, core growth areas. \par  \par (iii)    Investor demand and appetite for consumer finance-related businesses \par In light of the global economic turbulence and regional shifts in consumer spending \par behaviours over the past few years, the Company anticipated that investor sentiment \par toward consumer finance-related businesses would remain relatively weak in the \par immediate future. Furthermore, VCFC's asset value would remain inherently \par vulnerable to volatile market conditions, evolving credit cycles, and fluctuating interest \par rate environments. Rather than run the potential risk of exposing the Group to such \par potentially compounding macroeconomic uncertainties, which may severely constrain \par growth prospects and investment returns, the Company took the view that a strategic \par exit would best protect and maximise shareholder value. \par  \par (iv)     The Group's strategy to streamline its business and continue its existing focus on AI \par and technology-related operations; re-allocation of resources to AI and technology \par businesses \par The Company considered that the Disposal was consistent with the Group's broader \par strategy to streamline its business and continue its existing focus on AI and technology \par operations. The Disposal formed part of the Group's strategic positioning, including in \par particular AI and technology businesses, and enabled the Group to optimise its business \par portfolio and corporate focus. \par  \par     Concentrating management attention and operational resources on businesses that align \par more closely with this long-term strategy would be expected to enhance strategic \par execution and operational efficiency over time. The Company therefore considered that \par this re-allocation of resources would better position the Group to capture higher growth \par opportunities arising from increasing enterprise demand for AI and technology \par businesses. \par  \par Source: Hong Kong Exchanges & Clearing \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 22, 2026 10:51 ET (14:51 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Document DJDN000020260622em6m00244\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
