\par\par \b \uc2 Micron Earnings Will Likely Whip Up AI Frenzy. Why That's Bad for Markets. -- Barrons.com\b0\par\par\uc2 1,658 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 June 2026\par 14:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2026, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 It's sometimes hard to remember there's anything driving the market apart from \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But investors should look beyond AI darling \uc2 Micron Technology\uc2  when assessing the health of the U.S. economy this week.\par \par \uc2 Micron is likely to put up stunning numbers when it reports after the market closes Wednesday. Incredible demand for memory chips is likely to mean revenue nearly quadrupling and earnings growing at an even sharper pace. A strong report could give the AI-driven tech rally another boost.\par \par \uc2 But what's good news for Micron isn't necessarily positive for the wider economy. Soaring memory costs are driving up prices of smartphones, PCs, and other goods -- even Apple is feeling the pinch. It's an effect that might turn up in May's personal consumption expenditures report on Thursday. The core PCE, which excludes volatile food and energy prices, is expected to rise at a 0.4% monthly rate, compared with 0.2% in April. Watch the durable goods component for evidence of rising hardware costs.\par \par \uc2 Further clues about how inflation is affecting American consumers might come from shipping giant \uc2 FedEx\uc2  and cruise operator Carnival, both set to post results on Tuesday. \uc2 FedEx\uc2  volumes are a bellwether for the economy as a whole, although figures could be messy in the first report since the spinoff of the \uc2 FedEx Freight\uc2  business. Carnival could give an indication on how soon the tentative U.S.-Iran peace deal can lead to relief on fuel costs and travel-spending trends.\par \par \uc2 FedEx\uc2  and Carnival aren't likely to rack up 800% gains like Micron has over the past 12 months. But investors need to be wary of assuming traditionally cyclical businesses such as memory chips can sustain the market ever. A little bit of consumer cheer alongside the AI exhilaration wouldn't go amiss.\par \par \uc2 -- Adam Clark\par \par \uc2 Barron's Live: Retail sales are rising, but retail stocks are struggling. What gives? Join Barron's Live today at noon for a deep dive into the consumer economy and retailing business, and a discussion of retail stocks. Senior Managing Editor Lauren R. Rublin speaks with \uc2 Dana Telsey\uc2 , an award-winning retail analyst and founder of \uc2 Telsey Advisory Group\uc2 , and Senior Writer Teresa Rivas, who covers \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , \uc2 Costco\uc2 , \uc2 Tapestry\uc2 , and other retail industry leaders. Sign up here.\par \par \uc2 Get more of the journalism you love. Choose Barron's as a preferred source in \uc2 Google\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Oil in Focus With U.S.-Iran Talks Underway in Switzerland\par \par \uc2 The talks between the U.S. and Iran faced a potential complication after negotiators convened in Switzerland to hammer out a permanent deal. Word from the Iranian side suggested its leaders weren't happy with President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's threats to renew bombing if Iran doesn't curb activity by \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -- Qatar and Pakistan announced that the U.S. and Iran had agreed to a \par       mechanism to ensure the termination of military operations in Lebanon \par       during talks in Switzerland, in a joint statement early Monday. They \par       added that a line of communication had been formed to avoid incidents and \par       miscommunication and allow the safe passage of commercial vessels through \par       the Strait of Hormuz. \par  \par    -- Some reports over the weekend said the talks stalled because of Trump's \par       threat, while others said talks had taken a break but were still expected \par       to continue. \par  \par    -- Trump said on his own social-media platform Sunday that he would "hit \par       Iran very hard again" if Iran's proxies in Lebanon weren't stopped from \par       causing trouble. He told Fox News the U.S. may have to take over the \par       Strait of Hormuz, which Iran said it shut down on Saturday. \par  \par    -- Energy Secretary \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chris Wright\f28 \uc2 \f2  said 67 ships went through the strait on \par       Saturday, with oil and oil product volumes about equal to where things \par       stood before the war. Wright told ABC News there are three channels \par       through the strait, and one needs to be cleared of mines. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What's Next: Oil prices were falling to start the week but they remain volatile as tensions between the U.S. and Iran appear fraught. Still, as of Monday, Brent crude, the international benchmark, was up about 30% for the year. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -- Liz Moyer, Abby Schultz   and Callum Keown \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Housing Is Out of Its Deep Freeze But Not Everywhere\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Home buyers are coming back -- at least in some rising hot spots. But while buyers and sellers are back at the table in some markets, others are still locked in place. A seasonally adjusted index tracking contract signings rose in May to its highest level this year, but the landscape isn't uniform. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -- Nationally, home buyers and sellers in May struck the most tentative \par       deals since November, according to the National Association of Realtors. \par       Even with above-6% mortgage rates, that buyer rush indicates pent-up \par       demand, \f28 \uc2 \f2 NAR\f28 \uc2 \f2 's chief economist \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lawrence Yun\f28 \uc2 \f2  said. Consumers may see rates \par       as a new normal. \par  \par    -- In the Northeast, where inventory is low, there has been faster home \par       price growth but slower home sales for several months, but now it is \par       showing more buyer contract signings, Yun noted. In the south, contract \par       signings remained higher than other regions -- but the improvement \par       compared with last year was muted. \par  \par    -- Pending sales in the west are most challenged. In Arizona's greater \par       Phoenix area, "it just feels like it's the spring that didn't happen," \par       says DeLex Realty's Wendy Herst. Single-family houses in May spent 73 \par       days on the market, according to data published by Phoenix Realtors. \par  \par    -- Contract signings in the West saw a modest 1.2% increase from a year ago, \par       the slightest of all four regions tracked by the NAR, and the national \par       trend higher could continue. If last week's retreat in the 10-year \par       Treasury yield holds, mortgage rates could drift lower. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What's Next: The hope for lower mortgage rates sent the stocks of home builders higher. The iShares U.S. Home Construction exchange-traded fund reached its highest close since April. It is up nearly 11% over the past 12 months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Shaina Mishkin\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Next Hot Quantum Stock Is an AI Data Center Play\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European quantum computing leader IQM is on the verge of its biggest milestone yet. But first Real Asset Acquisition Corp. shareholders have to approve a merger with the Finnish company. If they do, it would be the first time a European quantum company trades on a major U.S. exchange. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -- IQM has sold 23 gate-based machines, making it a leader in on-premises \par       system sales, ahead of IBM, whose massive fleet of quantum computers is \par       primarily accessible only over the cloud. IQM has also started making \par       sales in the data-center market. \par  \par    -- In April, Poland's Galaxy Systemy Informatyczne became the first private \par       company to purchase an IQM computer. IQM CEO Jan Goetz told Barron's he \par       believes pairing the company's superconducting quantum technology with \par       classical processors will not only speed up the time to solution, but cut \par       power consumption. \par  \par    -- In the long term, quantum systems are expected to live alongside \par       classical processors in data centers to solve complex problems. While IQM \par       has experienced early success, the system is still taking shape. Like \par       other pure plays, IQM has yet to turn a yearly profit and continues to \par       burn through cash. \par  \par    -- To offset these losses, IQM is leaning on a familiar quantum-sector \par       playbook: a blank-check merger, which usually guarantees a faster route \par       to the public market and much-needed capital. Listing on the Nasdaq is \par       also IQM's play for a U.S. presence. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What's Next: All signs point to Real Asset Acquisition shareholders approving the transaction. Institutional investors have shown confidence in the deal; in June, strong demand pushed the private investment in public equity commitment to $146 million from an original $134 million.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Mackenzie Tatananni\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Disney's 'Toy Story 5' Notches Year's Biggest Movie Debut\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Walt Disney and Pixar Animation Studios' Toy Story 5 shattered already enormous expectations over the holiday weekend, notching the biggest movie debut of the year and continuing this year's brisk summer movie momentum. Overall box office sales for the weekend were estimated at $230 million. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -- For Toy Story 5, domestic ticket sales of $160 million came in at the \par       high end of expectations, including $71 million on Friday's Juneteenth \par       holiday, and easily surpassed the $131.7 million that Universal Pictures \par       and Nintendo's The Super Mario Galaxy Movie generated in April. \par  \par    -- For Hollywood, the estimated $1.82 billion in summer domestic box office \par       sales through Sunday is 15.2% ahead of last summer and only 1.9% below \par       2019. The estimated $4.46 billion in domestic box office sales so far \par       this year are 14.2% higher than last year. \par  \par    -- Toy Story 5, made on a $250 million budget that doesn't include marketing, \par       is widely expected to outsell Toy Story 3 and Toy Story 4, which each \par       sold about $1.07 billion worldwide. That doesn't include revenue from \par       toys, theater concessions, and LeapFrog's Toy Story-themed tablet \par       Lilypad. \par  \par    -- The 11.2 million people who saw Toy Story 5 constituted 69% of the \par       national foot traffic and 97% of the premium foot traffic, meaning those \par       who saw it on IMAX and premium large-format screens, according to \par       EntTelligence. Audience members skewed female (64%). \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What's Next: There's still a packed calendar of film debuts ahead. Warner Bros.' Supergirl premieres Friday, while Universal's Minions & Monsters opens July 1). Universal's The Odyssey and Sony Pictures' Spider-Man: Brand New Day are also slated for July .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Janet H. Cho\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Newsletter edited by Liz Moyer, Patrick O'Donnell, Callum Keown\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This content was created by Barron's, which is operated by Dow Jones & Co. Barron's is published independently from Dow Jones Newswires and The Wall Street Journal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 22, 2026 08:06 ET (12:06 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Document DJDN000020260622em6m001hd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
