\par\par \b \uc2 Heard on the Street: \uc2 Apple\uc2  War Chest Can't Win Memory War -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 914 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 June 2026\par 08:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par B7\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2026, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The iPhone maker's clout as a buyer has taken a major hit from the \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial-intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2  boom\par \par \uc2 By Dan Gallagher\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2  can afford the memory chips that it needs. But the fact that it's getting harder says something significant about the severity of the memory crunch -- and how one of the world's richest companies can still end up at a disadvantage.\par \par \uc2 After months of speculation about what the consumer-electronics giant might do in the face of soaring costs for memory chips, \uc2 Apple\uc2  now says it will be raising prices on its products. Chief Executive \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  told The Wall Street Journal that the company has been trying to mitigate the "huge increases" in memory costs without passing them on to its customers, but "the situation has become unsustainable."\par \par \uc2 That is a humbling admission from a company long accustomed to running the table on its suppliers. \uc2 Apple\uc2 's prowess at managing its supply chain has allowed the company to command superior gross profit margins relative to other major sellers of tech hardware.\par \par \uc2 But booming AI demand is squeezing out production for the types of memory chips needed for devices like PCs and smartphones. That has sent prices soaring. Market research firm TrendForce says prices for the type of DRAM -- dynamic random-access memory -- used in high-end smartphones will surge as much as 83% in the current quarter compared with three months ago.\par \par \uc2 The AI craze is also costing \uc2 Apple\uc2  its position as the biggest kid on the procurement block. \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , which buys up memory for the AI supercomputer systems it designs, is on track to surpass \uc2 Apple\uc2  in annual free cash flow this year. Wall Street also expects the AI chip maker to be generating more than double \uc2 Apple\uc2 's annual free cash flow in two years, according to consensus estimates from Visible Alpha. "We're the only chip company that buys directly tens of billions of dollars of DRAM from all the DRAM makers," \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  CEO \uc2 Jensen Huang\uc2  said at a conference earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 That might seem like an odd bragging point. But \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 's gross profit margins are now in the mid-70% range compared with the high 40% range for \uc2 Apple\uc2 . And the iPhone maker's business model also puts it at a structural disadvantage relative to the other tech giants who buy up memory for their cloud-computing services and can thus treat those purchases as a capital expense, depreciating the cost over time.\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2 's memory purchases go directly under its cost of goods sold. So significantly higher component costs can weigh down gross margins if the price on its products stays the same. Those margins are closely watched by Wall Street analysts who expect the number to keep trending up -- despite the well-known memory shortage. \uc2 Apple\uc2 's gross margin is expected to top 48% in the current fiscal year for the first time since 1990, according to consensus estimates from FactSet.\par \par \uc2 Cook declined to specify which products would be affected, or by how much. In a note Thursday, \uc2 Wamsi Mohan\uc2  of \uc2 BofA Securities\uc2  said he was already expecting iPhone prices to go up by $100, but he now expects an additional $100 hike on iPhone Pro models.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Apple\uc2 's devices already occupy a relatively premium tier, with its various iPhone configurations alone now averaging a price tag over $1,100. So any significant price increase runs the risk of hurting demand just as \uc2 Apple\uc2  needs its hardware products to drive its \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial-intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts. At its developers conference earlier this month, \uc2 Apple\uc2  disclosed that some of the most powerful new AI capabilities coming out this year will only be available on three of its most recent iPhone models -- which already carry an average price tag of $1,369.\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2 's war chest of $62 billion in cash net of debt is still a major asset. But the company also returns more than $100 billion a year in share buybacks and dividends, while new incoming CEO \uc2 John Ternus\uc2  promised in \uc2 Apple\uc2 's last earnings call to maintain "deep thoughtfulness, deliberateness and discipline" on the company's financial decision-making.\par \par \uc2 The memory crunch will still prove a major test for Ternus, even if his predecessor owns the unpopular decision to jack up prices. \uc2 Apple\uc2  is the only trillion-dollar tech company without a major foothold in AI now, and growing its potential market will require more devices with enough DRAM to power those experiences. And memory costs won't be going down anytime soon. \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  analyst Melissa Weathers said the shortage of DRAM "could persist well into 2028 and potentially beyond," in a report Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 In its last earnings call, \uc2 Apple\uc2  announced a change in its cash-management philosophy by no longer aiming for neutral levels of net cash. Bernstein analyst Mark Newman said this could "preserve dry powder" for larger investments in AI, and even major M&A deals that \uc2 Apple\uc2  has long avoided. But just getting the memory chips it needs to get its latest products out the door won't come cheap.\par \par \uc2 Write to Dan Gallagher at dan.gallagher@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (June 22, 2026).\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 22, 2026 02:33 ET (06:33 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Document DJDN000020260622em6m0004t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
